:R-HSA-2142850  a           obo:GO_0030213 , owl:NamedIndividual ;
        rdfs:comment        "Hyaluronan (hyaluronic acid, HA) is composed of repeating disaccharide units of glucuronic acid and N-acetylglucosamine [-4GlcAb1-3GlcNAcb1-]. It is synthesized in the cell membrane by adding monosaccharides to the reducing end of the chain using the precursors UDP-glucuronic acid and UDP-N-acetylglucosamine in the presence of Mg2+. The integral membrane dual-action glycosyltransferase proteins hyaluronan synthases, of which vertebrates have three types (HAS1-3), catalyze these monosaccharide additions. Unlike other GAGs, HA is synthesized as a free glycan, not attached to a protein (Laurent 1987, Weigel & DeAngelis, 2007). As HA is synthesised it is extruded from the cell by an ABC-type transporter into the extracellular medium." ;
        rdfs:label          "Hyaluronan biosynthesis and export" ;
        dc:contributor      "Reviewed: D'Eustachio, P, 2012-03-28" , "Authored: Jassal, B, 2012-02-24" , "GOC:reactome_curators" , "Edited: Jassal, B, 2012-02-24" ;
        dc:date             "2023-06-21" ;
        pav:providedBy      "https://reactome.org" ;
        oboInOwl:hasDbXref  "R-HSA-2142850" ;
        skos:narrower       <http://model.geneontology.org/R-HSA-2142845/R-HSA-2142845> ;
        skos:note           "org.biopax.paxtools.model.level3.Pathway" .

